<header><p class="st-kicker">other · Iberia</p><h1 class="st-title">Banu Qasi</h1><p class="st-meta">714 CE – 929 CE (approx.) · capital Tudela</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Muslim dynasty of Visigothic descent that dominated the upper Ebro for two centuries, allied by blood with the kings of Pamplona.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Banu Qasi claimed descent from a Visigothic count called Cassius who converted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> after the conquest of 711 to 714, keeping his lands in the upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ebro-river">Ebro</a> valley. As muwallads, native Muslims, they became the leading family of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-march">Upper March</a>, based at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tudela">Tudela</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>They balanced between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cordoba">Córdoba</a>, the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/franks">Franks</a> and the Christian kingdoms, intermarrying with the Arista family of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pamplona">Pamplona</a>, whose founder <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inigo-arista">Íñigo Arista</a> was a half-brother of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/musa-ibn-musa">Musa ibn Musa</a>. Musa ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tudela">Tudela</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zaragoza">Zaragoza</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huesca">Huesca</a> and was called the third king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ordono-i-of-asturias">Ordoño I of Asturias</a> defeated him at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-albelda">Albelda</a> in 859. Later family members fought Córdoba, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tujibids">Tujibids</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-navarre">Navarre</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Weakened by family divisions and wars with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sancho-garces-i-of-pamplona">Sancho Garcés I of Pamplona</a>, the dynasty lost <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tudela">Tudela</a> and its lands. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abd-al-rahman-iii">Abd al-Rahman III</a> ended its power in the 920s, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tujibids">Tujibid</a> family took over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-march">Upper March</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/banu-qasi.jpg" alt="Banu Qasi" width="320" height="260"><figcaption>Image: Sugaar at English Wikipedia · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kingdom_of_Pamplona_(c._925).gif" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>714 CE – 929 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/8th-century">8th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Tudela</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Musa ibn Musa, Muhammad ibn Lubb, Lubb ibn Muhammad</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Arabic</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Banu Qasi of the Upper March</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=822&amp;focus=banu-qasi" aria-label="Banu Qasi on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4641 680 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Banu Qasi lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M4961 936L4881 1071M4961 936L4881 1071"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M4881 1071h0M4881 1071h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4961 936h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4961 936h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Tudela. <a href="/atlas?year=822&amp;focus=banu-qasi">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

